<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- **Off, and this is the one application here that turns it off.**
	     The sandbox exists to stop a program touching files nobody handed it,
	     and this program's entire purpose is to open a level document
	     somewhere in this repository, change it and write it back over
	     itself. Under the sandbox that is `PathAccessException: Operation not
	     permitted` on a path that is plainly there.

	     The alternative Apple offers is a file picker and
	     `files.user-selected.read-write`, which grants access one file at a
	     time to whatever a person chose in a dialogue. That is the right
	     answer for something shipped to strangers; this is a developer tool
	     that runs from `flutter run` beside the levels it edits, is not
	     signed, is not distributed and would fail its own first launch under
	     the rule. The three games keep the sandbox, because a game reads its
	     own bundle and nothing else. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<false/>
</dict>
</plist>
